so that \(\mathbf{u}\) has indeed a vector potential \(\psi\). On the other hand, \(\psi\) cannot satisfy the boundary condition (5.39) because it implies \((\mathbf{curl}\,\psi)\cdot\mathbf{n}|_{\Gamma}=0\). However, it is possible to prescribe condition (5.38). Thus a reasonable choice for the space of vector potentials is:

\[\Psi_{2}=\{\phi\in L^{2}(\Omega)^{3};\,\mathrm{div}\,\phi\in H^{1}(\Omega),\, \mathbf{curl}\,\phi\in H^{1}(\Omega)^{3},\,\phi\cdot\mathbf{n}|_{\Gamma}=0\}.\]

Likewise, let us take \(\Psi_{1}\) as space of test functions. Then consider the biharmonic problem:

_Find \(\psi\) in \(\Psi_{2}\) with \(\mathbf{curl}\,\psi|_{\Gamma}=\mathbf{g}\) such that_

(5.50) \[v(\Delta\psi,\Delta\phi)=\langle\mathbf{f},\mathbf{curl}\,\phi\rangle\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\forall\phi\in\Psi_{1}.\]

It is easy to check that this problem has a unique solution but the relation between \(\psi\) and \(\mathbf{u}\) is not altogether trivial because (5.43) no longer holds for all \(\phi\) in \(\Psi_{1}\). However, observe that \(\psi\) and the divergence-free potential \(\psi_{0}\) of \(\mathbf{u}\) in \(\Psi_{1}(\mathbf{curl}\,\psi_{0}=\mathbf{u},\mathrm{div}\,\psi_{0}=0,\psi_{0} \cdot\mathbf{n}|_{\Gamma}=0)\) both satisfy:

\[v(\mathbf{curl}\,\mathbf{curl}\,\psi,\mathbf{curl}\,\mathbf{curl}\,\phi)= \langle\mathbf{f},\mathbf{curl}\,\phi\rangle\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\forall\phi \in\Psi_{1}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\text{with}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\mathrm{div}\,\phi=0.\]

Thus, setting

\[\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{curl}(\psi-\psi_{0})\in V,\]

we derive \(\mathbf{curl}\,\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{0}\) and hence \(\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{0}\). Therefore \(\psi\) is a vector potential of \(\mathbf{u}\), but it is not necessarily divergence-free.

**Theorem 5.6**.: _Let \(\Omega\) be like in Lemma 5.1 and let \(\mathbf{g}\) satisfy (5.49). The biharmonic problem (5.50) has a unique solution \(\psi\) in \(\Psi_{2}\) and \(\mathbf{curl}\,\psi\) is the unique solution of the non-homogeneous Stokes problem (5.12)._

_Remark 5.11_.: The divergence of \(\psi\) is the solution \(\lambda\) of the Neumann's problem:

\[\Delta\lambda=0\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,